Debian 13 update (2026-10-01)
----------------------------
See QUALIFICATION-DEBIAN13 for the native amd64/arm64 package qualification.
Ubuntu-only scope statements below describe the initial packaging checkpoint.

Debian/Ubuntu packaging for the VSS modules
=========================================

qore-vss-module contains VssLoader and VssDataProvider sources, AOT-compiled
modules, provider resources and translation catalogs. The runtime package is
Architecture: any because qcc emits native code even though this project has
no C++ sources. qore-vss-module-doc contains both HTML API references and the
complete upstream test scripts/data. Debhelper creates detached debug symbols.

Build prerequisites and supported scope
--------------------------------------

Use an isolated native build environment with the packaged Qore runtime/SDK
and Build-Depends from debian/control. The qualification target is Ubuntu
26.04 amd64 with Qore 3.0 development packages. Other releases/architectures
need their own matching SDK and native build/test run. Cross builds are rejected
because Qore/qcc execute during compilation and documentation generation.

JSON and YAML are included with the required Qore 3.0 runtime. Tests explicitly
load both before running, so optional compatibility paths in the upstream
sources cannot hide missing package dependencies. No service or network account
is needed; all catalogs and telemetry inputs are local test fixtures.

The build preserves distribution hardening flags and qcc debug information.
QCC_JOBS=1 fixes AOT partitioning independently of build job count; independent
targets still run in parallel. dh_strip is wrapped to preserve Qore dependency,
source-location and optional-module trailers. dwz is disabled for these AOT
objects because it does not support all LLVM DWARF forms. Runtime/libqore SONAME
dependencies derive from the installed SDK; other libraries use dh_shlibdeps.
The package is not Multi-Arch: same because it requires the native interpreter.

The module implementation is MIT. The curated test/data/vss_v6_subset.yaml
fixture derives from COVESA VSS v6.0 and is MPL-2.0, including its modifications.
It is shipped in editable source form with attribution and the upstream license
in COPYING.MPL-2.0; debian/copyright records this separately. Other test data
are local fixtures. No full upstream vehicle catalog is bundled.

Source and binary builds
------------------------

Use a new version for each changed upload. This snapshot precedes version 1.0.0.
From a clean committed checkout:

    version=1.0.0~git20260928.1
    mkdir -p ../vss-package-work
    git archive --format=tar --prefix="qore-vss-module-$version/" \
        HEAD -- . ':!debian' | xz > \
        "../vss-package-work/qore-vss-module_$version.orig.tar.xz"
    tar -xf "../vss-package-work/qore-vss-module_$version.orig.tar.xz" \
        -C ../vss-package-work
    cp -a debian "../vss-package-work/qore-vss-module-$version/"
    cd "../vss-package-work/qore-vss-module-$version"
    dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc

Extract the .dsc with dpkg-source -x. With dependencies preinstalled, build
unprivileged with networking disabled:

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

Architecture-only builds (-B) need no Doxygen; independent builds (-A) produce
HTML documentation. Both DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nodoc and DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=nodoc
suppress documentation; the profile also omits the doc package. Do not use
nocheck for qualification.

Qualification and publication
-----------------------------

Run all seven suites against the exact compiled modules, installed packages
away from checkout paths, and source modules separately. A fresh qcc executable
loads a VSS catalog, validates telemetry, converts units and uses the provider
factory. Verify installed resources/catalogs and AOT trailer parity, all standard
locale translations, source/binary Lintian, hardening/debug symbols, minimal
installation without the SDK, docs/debug coinstallation, dpkg -V, purge/reinstall,
and byte reproducibility at different paths and parallelism levels.
Retain exact source/package hashes, dependency versions, audit and test logs.
Testing PPA publication is separate from approval for a final general release.
